From 846a8014233991b413fa3bdb1a813fa5988cbae8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmitriy Andreychenko <dmitriy.andreychenko@flant.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 17:48:12 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] ipam: pin a pod's address with cni.cilium.io/ipAddress

Deckhouse Virtualization needs a pod to be able to request a specific address:

    cni.cilium.io/ipAddress: 10.10.10.10

The driving case is VM live migration. DVP migrates a virtual machine by running
a second pod for it on the target node while the first is still running on the
source node, and both pods carry the VM's address. Two consequences shape this
patch:

  - the pinned address is not necessarily part of the local node's allocation
    CIDR. With ipam=kubernetes each node allocates from its own podCIDR, so on
    the migration target the VM's address belongs to the *source* node's CIDR.
    A VM's address may also come from a subnet unrelated to the pod subnet
    altogether. There is therefore no CIDR to validate a request against.
  - two pods on different nodes legitimately hold the same address for the
    duration of the migration. Which of them owns it cluster-wide is decided
    separately, by the pod-common-ip-priority label.

So allocatePinnedIP() first tries the ordinary specific-IP path, and treats
ipallocator's ErrNotInRange as an expected outcome rather than a failure: an
address this node's allocator cannot represent is accepted, but left out of the
local bitmap, which has no slot for it. Every other error propagates --
ErrAllocated in particular, so a second pod on the *same* node asking for an
address already in use is still refused. For a foreign address, where the bitmap
cannot detect the duplicate, the IP owner map does; releaseIPOwner() already
clears it, and ipallocator.Range.Release() ignores out-of-range addresses, so
the release path needed no change.

The pre-1.20 version of this patch achieved the same acceptance by deleting the
range check from ipallocator.Range.Allocate outright. Since Range.contains
reports offset 0 for anything out of range, that aliased every foreign address
onto the first address of the node's own CIDR -- consuming a real address, and
making the second such pod on a node collide with the first, which is why it had
to delete the ErrAllocated check as well and thereby lost duplicate detection
for ordinary addresses too. Leaving the bitmap alone for foreign addresses gets
the same behaviour without either loss.

The pod is read from the local-pods StateDB table, the same source
pkg/ipam/metadata uses for pool annotations. It cannot go through the metadata
manager itself: newIPAMMetadataManager() degrades to a stub for every IPAM mode
other than multi-pool, and Deckhouse runs ipam=kubernetes. Nor does it go
through k8sWatcher.GetCachedPod(), which performs the very same table lookup but
wraps it in WaitForCacheSync: that waits for the initial sync of all pods and not
for this pod, so it does not close the race below, while it does add a blocking
wait and a watcher dependency to a package that needs one for nothing else.

That table is fed by a watch-backed reflector committing in batches every
k8s.DefaultBufferWaitTime, so a CNI ADD can arrive before the pod appears in it:
kubelet learns of the pod from its own watch, and nothing orders the two.
resolveRequestedIP() waits on the table's watch channel for that specific pod,
bounded by podTableWaitTimeout, and on timeout allocates as upstream would while
logging a warning. Failing the ADD instead was considered and rejected: this code
sits on the path every pod on the node takes, and a stale or broken cache must
not be able to refuse address allocation node-wide. The residual cost is that a
pinned pod caught past the timeout comes up with an ordinary address, which is
why the warning is loud -- nothing else in the system reports that it happened.

The lookup runs outside allocatorMutex. That mutex is the agent's single global
IPAM lock, taken by every allocate and every release, so the region it covers
must hold in-memory allocator work only: no k8s reads, no channel waits, no I/O.
The exported AllocateNextFamily() and AllocateNextFamilyWithoutSyncUpstream()
therefore resolve the annotation before locking and pass the result down into
allocateNextFamily() as an argument.

Restored endpoints take a different path. endpointRestorer.allocateIPsLocked()
re-allocates the address recorded in the endpoint's state file, which for a
pinned pod may be foreign to this node and would fail with ErrNotInRange after an
agent restart, deleting a healthy endpoint. pinnedAddr() reads the pod's
annotation and pinnedOutOfRange() tolerates ErrNotInRange for exactly that
address, leaving every other restore failure untouched.

allocateIP() is split so allocatePinnedIP(), which runs with allocatorMutex held,
can reuse the body without re-locking.

Non-pod owners ("router", "health", "ingress") and a pod carrying no annotation
are "no request" and allocate normally, as is an annotation for the other address
family. A malformed address fails the CNI ADD rather than quietly handing out a
different one: that is the only outcome that fails an allocation, because it is
the only one where a request was made and cannot be honoured.

Upstream: https://github.com/cilium/cilium/pull/24098
Test: ~/src/kind/d8-1.20-tests/001-request-ip/run.sh
